MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

--dspark-draft-batch (CUDA): the whole MTP draft chain in one submission via
device-side token feedback (embed_tokens from the previous step's top id,
GPU top1-top2 margin kernel, single readback).  Measured neutral on GB10
(19.4 vs 19.7 tok/s over 3 interleaved reps): the per-step synchronize it
removes (~1-2 ms) is repaid by evals that run past the early-margin stop.
Batching round chains as well (DS4_DSPARK_ROUND_BATCH=1) is a clear loss
(17.8): rounds gate on the probe and truncate earlier, so the wasted evals
dominate.  Draft-during-verify overlap proper is dependency-blocked: the
chain needs the verifier's committed hidden state and next greedy token.

--dspark-markov FILE + --dspark-markov-fit OUT: offline confident-trigram
table (407k-token corpus -> 6349 trigrams kept at count>=4, majority>=2/3),
chained after the local suffix-ngram.  Measured a loss on every prompt class
(code 18.7->17.5, json 19.6->19.1, prose 14.9->14.7) even when gated to
local-miss-only and capped at 3 tokens: rejected rows past the accept point
still cost ~15 ms each and raw-trigram acceptance is far below the ~25%
break-even.  DSpark's real Markov head is trained on hidden states, not
token trigrams; the honest conclusion is that an offline table cannot
substitute for it.  Kept for reproduction; off by default.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
